# Writing VBA tests

The canonical guide for *authoring* a test in the VCS add-in or any Access
database using the VCS test runner. For the testing layers, the round-trip fixture
harness, and where results land, see [docs/testing-strategy.md](../../docs/testing-strategy.md);
for running and filtering, [docs/agent-test-runs.md](../../docs/agent-test-runs.md);
for the web UI and bridge, [docs/web-test-runner.md](../../docs/web-test-runner.md).

Tests assert with `TestAssert condition, "optional context"`, a drop-in
replacement for `Debug.Assert`. The context string identifies which assertion
failed. Under the test runner `TestAssert` reports back to the add-in; run
standalone (F5 or Immediate Window) it falls back to `Debug.Assert`.

## Standard module tests

Parameterless `Public Sub` procedures in a module identified as a test module:

```vba
Public Sub TestSomeBehavior()
    TestAssert MyFunction(42) = 84, "MyFunction should double input"
End Sub
```

## Class module tests (preferred for new tests)

Class modules get setup/teardown for free and each test method runs against a **fresh
instance**: `Class_Initialize` runs before each test method, `Class_Terminate` after it.

## Discovery rules

A module is a **test module** if it is a standard module (`vbext_ct_StdModule`) or
standalone class module (`vbext_ct_ClassModule`), AND either carries a
`'@Folder("...Tests...")` annotation (when the project uses `@Folder` anywhere) or
has `Test` in its name (case-insensitive fallback). Within one, a procedure is a
**test** if it is a `Public Sub` with zero parameters — or, in class modules, a
`Public Sub` or `Public Function` with zero parameters, excluding `Class_Initialize`
and `Class_Terminate`. Keep a helper out by making it `Private` or giving it a parameter.

## Tagging

`'@Tag("name")` annotations categorize tests for filtering. Tags are case-insensitive
and appear in the JSON results under a `"tags"` array. Module-level tags sit in the
first ~30 lines, before any procedure, and apply to every test in the module;
procedure-level tags go at the top of the body, before any `Dim`. Both sets merge.
## Global suite hooks

Optional **once-per-run** setup/teardown in the target project's `modTestAssert`:
`GlobalTestSetup` runs before the first test, `GlobalTestTeardown` after all tests
and the results JSON. They ship as empty stubs, are skipped when absent or when a
filter matches nothing, and their errors are non-fatal — teardown still runs.
`Class_Initialize` / `Class_Terminate` nest inside.

## Conventions

- Name test modules with `Test` in the name (`modTestEncoding`, `clsTestQueryParser`).
- Use `'@Folder("Tests")` for explicit discovery when the project has annotations.
- Use `Option Private Module` on standard test modules to keep test subs out of
  the macro list — the runner can still call them.
- One assertion concept per test method; use `context` to distinguish assertions.
- The runner auto-maintains `TestClassFactory` in `modTestAssert`, one `Select Case`
  entry per test class. Do not hand-edit between the markers.
- Do not add per-test `Operation.InteractionMode = eimSilent` guards; the runner
  owns root interaction policy (see below).

## Which project a test runs in

The *installed* add-in drives the run and owns the operation; test procedures
execute in the project being tested. For this repo those are two loaded copies of
the same project, so `Operation` inside a test is a different, idle instance whose
`Status`, `InteractionMode`, and `Attended` say nothing about the run. What every
hosted project can see is `modTestAssert.TestRunActive`, true during the run.

## Four ways a test can sabotage its own run

- **Never call `Operation.TryBeginRoot`** (or legacy `Begin`/`Finish`) on the
  session operation. It can be refused with a dialog or destroy the active runner.
  Test lifecycle behavior through a private `New clsOperation`; see
  `modTestOperationLifecycle`.
- **Never call `Log.Error`** on the session log. The runner reports any error
  logged during a test as a failure. Assert on the decision instead.
- **Never use `Err.Raise` in a test procedure**, including cleanup rethrows; it can
  open a blocking VBA error dialog. Trap and report instead, as below.
- **Never open a prompt.** Assert `PromptWouldDisplay` is false; call `MsgBox2`
  only where suppression is already established.

## Trapping errors in a test

Trapping must be **on at every line** of a test procedure. `On Error GoTo 0` is
banned: it disables trapping, so the next failure — including one inside
`TestAssert` — breaks into a modal dialog and stalls the whole run.
`On Error Resume Next` over a whole body is the opposite mistake, running on
through the broken state until follow-on errors bury the real one. Trap to a
handler, clean up unconditionally, then report:

```vba
    On Error GoTo ErrHandler
    ' ... fallible work and TestAssert calls ...

CleanUp:
    On Error Resume Next
    ' ... restore saved options, delete temp objects ...
    If lngErr <> 0 Then TestAssert False, "context " & lngErr & ": " & strErr
    Exit Sub

ErrHandler:
    lngErr = Err.Number
    strErr = Err.Description
    Resume CleanUp
```

Cleanup stays under `On Error Resume Next` to the end; the state is per-procedure
and does not leak to the caller. To end a tolerated scope mid-body — one statement
whose failure *is* the thing under test — go back to `On Error GoTo ErrHandler`.
`modTestTestProcedures` fails any discovered test that uses either banned construct.
